---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import elaraCharacter from '@/assets/blog/aether/elara-character.png';
import elaraScene from '@/assets/blog/aether/elara-scene.png';
import PostLayout from '@/layouts/PostLayout.astro';
import Architecture from '@/components/blog/aether/Architecture.astro';
import SceneGraph from '@/components/blog/aether/SceneGraph.astro';
import ArcTimeline from '@/components/blog/aether/ArcTimeline.astro';
import ToneMapper from '@/components/blog/aether/ToneMapper.astro';
import ResultsChart from '@/components/blog/aether/ResultsChart.astro';
import { posts } from '@/data/posts';
import '@/styles/viz.css';

const post = posts.find((p) => p.slug === 'aether-weaver')!;
const themes = { light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' } as const;

const codePrompt = `def narrator_prompt(story, arc, tones, graph):
    stage = arc.stage_at(story.progress)
    return "\\n\\n".join([
        story.user_inputs,                      # premise, characters, setting, style
        "Story so far: " + story.summary,
        f"Stage: {stage.name}. Goal: {stage.goal}",
        "Mood: " + tones.directive(stage),      # from the Affective Tone Mapper
        "Current world state:\\n" + graph.describe(),
    ])`;

const codeDirector = `def apply(graph, changes):
    for node in changes.removed:
        graph.remove_node(node)                 # its edges go with it
    for a, rel, b in changes.new_edges:
        if rel == "IS AT":                      # nobody can be in two places
            graph.remove_edges(a, "IS AT")
            graph.nodes[a]["current location"] = b
        graph.add_edge(a, rel, b)
    for node, key, value in changes.attributes:
        graph.nodes[node][key] = value
    return graph.check()                        # problems go back to the Narrator`;

const codeStage = `STAGES = [("Exposition", 0.00), ("Rising Action", 0.25), ("Climax", 0.65),
          ("Falling Action", 0.80), ("Resolution", 0.90)]

def stage_at(progress):                         # scenes written / scenes planned
    return [name for name, start in STAGES if progress >= start][-1]`;

const codeModulate = `def combine(user_tones, stage):
    tones = list(user_tones)
    # A strongly negative stage turns the user's positive moods down a notch
    if any(t in NEGATIVE and level == "High" for t, level in stage.cues):
        tones = [(t, lower(level) if t in POSITIVE else level) for t, level in tones]
    return tones + stage.cues                   # the stage always adds its own cues`;
---
<PostLayout title={post.title} date={new Date(post.date)} description={post.description} readingMinutes={post.readingMinutes} ogImage={post.ogImage}>
  <p>
    If you have played with AI storytelling tools you have probably seen something like this. In the text, the
    heroine drops her lantern in the river; two pictures later she is holding it again. A character who is
    terrified on the page is calmly smiling in the illustration. And the soundtrack is vaguely "ominous" whatever
    is actually going on.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most of the time the culprit is the pipeline. The usual way to build these systems is a cascade: a language
    model writes the story, and then separate modules read the finished text and make pictures and sounds for
    it<a href="#ref-arif" class="cite">[2]</a><a href="#ref-seed" class="cite">[3]</a>. Each step only sees what
    the step before it wrote down, and nothing keeps track of the world the story is happening in: who is where,
    what they are holding, and what state things are in.
  </p>
  <p>
    Aether Weaver<a href="#ref-aw" class="cite">[1]</a> is my attempt at doing this differently. Rather than
    writing the story first and decorating it afterwards, it generates the text, a structured model of the
    story's world, the pictures and the sound cues together, one scene at a time, with each piece feeding the
    others. In this article I want to walk through how it works, using one story from the paper as a running
    example: Elara, who meets the spirit of an ancient tree called the Elderwood.
  </p>
  <p>
    The article is split into a few parts. First, an overview of the whole loop. Then one section for each of the
    three components that do the steering: the Director, which remembers the world; the Narrative Arc Controller,
    which keeps the plot on track; and the Affective Tone Mapper, which keeps the mood consistent across text,
    pictures and sound. After that I'll show how characters stay recognisable from picture to picture, what the
    expert evaluation found, and what I learned along the way. The code snippets are simplified Python sketches of
    the ideas rather than the production code.
  </p>
  <ul class="toc">
    <li><a href="#overview">Overview</a></li>
    <li><a href="#director">The Director</a></li>
    <li><a href="#arc">The Narrative Arc Controller</a></li>
    <li><a href="#tone">The Affective Tone Mapper</a></li>
    <li><a href="#characters">Keeping Characters Recognisable</a></li>
    <li><a href="#results">Does It Work?</a></li>
    <li><a href="#lessons">Lessons</a></li>
    <li><a href="#next">What's Next</a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="overview">Overview</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the set-up. Before a story starts, you give Aether Weaver a premise, optionally some characters and a
    setting, a narrative arc (a Classic Arc, a Tragedy, a Hero's Journey and so on), one to three moods with an
    intensity each ("Mystery: High", "Hope: Medium"), and a visual style. Then it writes the story one scene at a
    time.
  </p>

  <Architecture />

  <p>
    Five components take part in every scene. The <strong>Narrative Arc Controller</strong> works out where we are
    in the plot and what this scene should achieve. The <strong>Affective Tone Mapper</strong> turns the moods into
    concrete guidance. The <strong>Narrator</strong>, a large language model, writes the scene along with a list of
    sound cues. The <strong>Director</strong> reads what was written and updates its model of the world, or pushes
    back if something doesn't add up. Finally, the <strong>Visual Scene Generator</strong> combines all of this into
    one detailed image prompt and draws the scene.
  </p>
  <p>
    The key difference from a cascade is the loop between the Narrator and the Director. The Narrator doesn't just
    get "the story so far": every prompt also carries the current state of the world. Roughly, it is put together
    like this:
  </p>
  <Code code={codePrompt} lang="python" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
  <p>Each of those lines comes from a different component, so let's go through them in turn.</p>

  <h2 id="director">The Director</h2>
  <p>
    The Director's job is to remember the world. It keeps a <em>scene graph</em>: nodes for the things in the story
    (characters, objects, locations and events), directed edges for how they relate ("Elara IS AT the Elderwood"),
    and attributes on both ("Elara's mood: wonder and determination"). Scene graphs are a standard way of
    describing what is in an image in computer vision<a href="#ref-johnson" class="cite">[4]</a>. Here the graph
    describes what is in the story, and it changes as the story goes.
  </p>
  <p>Here is what the Director knew after the first scene of Elara's story, exactly as it came out of the system:</p>

  <SceneGraph />

  <p>
    After every scene, the Director turns the Narrator's prose into updates to this graph. It finds the
    characters, objects and places that are mentioned and adds any new ones. It extracts the relations between
    them: actions, spatial relations and causes. And it notices when something's state has changed (the paper's
    example is "The once gleaming artifact was now tarnished"). It also tracks rough relative positions, such as
    near, inside and on, so that things don't teleport unless the story says so.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then come a few consistency rules. If something leaves the story, its relations go with it. If a character
    moves, their old location is replaced, because nobody can be in two places at once. And if an update doesn't
    make sense, the Director flags it or sends a corrective prompt back to the Narrator. The core of it looks
    something like this:
  </p>
  <Code code={codeDirector} lang="python" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
  <p>
    The second view in the figure above shows these rules at work. It's my own illustration rather than an output
    from the paper: I wrote one more sentence for the story and applied the changes the rules call for. Elara's old
    IS AT edge is removed, a new one points to the Forgotten Shrine, and her location attribute is updated to match.
    It's a small thing, but it is exactly the kind of thing a cascade forgets. When the next picture is drawn, the
    graph says Elara is at the shrine, not still standing under the tree.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because the graph is plain structured data, a person can also read it and edit it. That turns out to be
    useful, and I'll come back to it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="arc">The Narrative Arc Controller</h2>
  <p>
    Ask a language model for a story with a beginning, a middle and an end and you'll usually get one. But ask it
    to keep writing scene after scene and it tends to meander. The Narrative Arc Controller fixes this with
    something very simple: a state machine. Each arc type (Classic Arc, Tragedy, Hero's Journey, Rags to Riches,
    Quest and Character Arc) is a sequence of stages, and each stage comes with a goal, some mood cues and a few
    keywords. Here is the Classic Arc:
  </p>

  <ArcTimeline />

  <p>
    The interesting design choice is when to move from one stage to the next. You could wait for an event ("the
    climax has happened"), or ask the Narrator whether it thinks the stage is finished. I went with the simplest
    thing that could work: each stage starts at a fixed fraction of the planned story. With ten planned scenes,
    the climax arrives about two thirds of the way through, whatever else is going on.
  </p>
  <Code code={codeStage} lang="python" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
  <p>
    This sounds almost too simple, but it worked well, and it is predictable, which matters when a person is
    co-writing with the system. The stage's goal goes straight into the Narrator's prompt as a directive, and its
    mood cues go to the Affective Tone Mapper.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tone">The Affective Tone Mapper</h2>
  <p>
    Mood is where text, pictures and sound most easily drift apart. "Mystery" means something to a language model,
    something else to an image generator, and something else again to whoever (or whatever) picks the sounds. The
    Affective Tone Mapper bridges this with a small knowledge base: for every mood, how to express it in words, in
    sound and in pictures, at three intensities.
  </p>

  <ToneMapper />

  <p>
    So for every scene the mapper produces three things: a directive for the Narrator, a list of sound cues and a
    list of visual cues. The difference shows up in the details. In the evaluation, a character's "growing dread"
    led Aether Weaver to suggest "low, rising dissonance" and "exaggerated heartbeat sounds", where the baseline
    offered generic cues like "ominous music".
  </p>
  <p>
    The mapper also listens to the arc. The current stage always adds its own mood cues, and a strongly negative
    stage turns your positive moods down a notch, so a story you asked to be joyful doesn't stay cheerful through
    its darkest moment:
  </p>
  <Code code={codeModulate} lang="python" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />

  <h2 id="characters">Keeping Characters Recognisable</h2>
  <p>
    Text is the easy part to keep consistent. Pictures are much harder, because every scene is drawn from scratch
    by a text-to-image model, and if the prompt just says "Elara" you get a different Elara every time. Aether Weaver
    tackles this with what the paper calls multimodal character grounding.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each character starts with a profile: name, description, personality, appearance and clothing. If you upload a
    portrait, that becomes the reference. If not, the system generates one from the profile, a full-body view and a
    close-up. Then a vision model looks at that image and writes back a much more detailed description of what the
    character actually looks like. From then on, whenever the character is in a scene, that description is added
    to the image prompt.
  </p>

  <figure class="post-figure aw-ground">
    <div class="aw-ground__row">
      <Image src={elaraCharacter} alt="Elara's reference image: a full-body view and a close-up of a red-haired character with green eyes, braids, a leafy green outfit, a wooden staff and a fox." width={560} class="aw-ground__img" />
      <span class="aw-ground__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      <Image src={elaraScene} alt="A scene with Elara and the fox standing before a huge glowing tree with a face, in a dark forest." width={560} class="aw-ground__img" />
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      Left: Elara's reference image, generated from her profile. A vision model describes it in detail, and that
      description goes into every image prompt she appears in. Right: a scene with Elara, drawn with that
      description in the prompt. Both images were generated by Aether Weaver (Figure 3 of the paper).
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    It helps a lot, but it isn't a guarantee: the image model still draws every scene from nothing, so small
    details can drift. That is one of the limitations I come back to at the end.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same structured data also keeps the writer in charge. You can edit any scene's text or its scene graph
    directly. When you save an edited scene, the Director re-reads it and updates the graph; if your edit
    introduces a new character, they get a profile of their own, and new visual and sound cues are generated to
    match. There is also a Plot Twist feature: pick one of the twists the model suggests, or write your own, and the
    Narrator builds the next scene around it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="results">Does It Work?</h2>
  <p>
    To evaluate it, I put together 10 story prompts across genres such as sci-fi and fantasy. Each came with one to
    three moods at chosen intensities, and a standard arc. The baseline was a cascade similar to Arif et
    al.<a href="#ref-arif" class="cite">[2]</a>: a language model writes the whole story first, then entity
    extraction feeds a generic image prompt and a simple soundscape description (if the text says "ominous", you
    get "ominous sounds"). There is no scene graph and no mood control.
  </p>
  <p>
    A panel of 10 experts (professional writers, game designers, and AI researchers who work on narrative systems)
    rated the results on a five-point scale:
  </p>

  <ResultsChart />

  <p>
    The pattern is what you would hope for, given where each component acts. Fluency barely moves: modern language
    models already write fluent prose, and both systems used one. The big gaps are in visual coherence (4.23
    against 2.85) and affective alignment (4.67 against 3.28), the two things the Director and the mood controls are
    there for. The experts' notes say the same: the baseline often forgot objects, put characters in inconsistent
    poses or places, and didn't update the state of things, and its emotional tone was often flat, or shifted
    without a reason.
  </p>
  <p>
    I want to be clear about what kind of evaluation this is: a small expert study, reported as average ratings. It
    tells you that people who know stories found the output clearly better. It doesn't count how often the system
    still gets things wrong. Measuring that directly, for example by counting continuity errors per thousand words,
    or by checking whether a story's emotional curve really follows the requested arc, would make a good next
    experiment.
  </p>

  <h2 id="lessons">Lessons</h2>
  <h3>Give the model a world, not just a history</h3>
  <p>
    A summary of the story so far tells a language model what happened. It doesn't tell it what is true now.
    Making the world state explicit, and giving a separate step the job of maintaining it, is the core idea of
    Aether Weaver, and visual coherence is where the biggest improvement showed up.
  </p>
  <h3>A little structure goes a long way</h3>
  <p>
    The arc controller is just a list of stages with a threshold each. Simple, predictable rules gave the plots a
    shape that the experts noticed: arc adherence went from 3.45 to 4.30.
  </p>
  <h3>Every medium needs its own vocabulary for a mood</h3>
  <p>
    Pass the word "mystery" to three different models and you get three unrelated ideas of mystery. Translating it
    once, into words, sounds and visual cues, keeps them in step.
  </p>
  <h3>Pictures forget faster than words</h3>
  <p>
    The text stayed consistent much more easily than the images. Most of the inconsistency that remains comes from
    drawing each scene from scratch.
  </p>
  <h3>Keep the writer in the loop</h3>
  <p>
    Making the scene graph readable and editable turns a black box into something a writer can steer, and it means
    a mistake can be fixed in one place instead of in every scene that follows.
  </p>

  <h2 id="next">What's Next</h2>
  <p>There is plenty left to do. These are the main gaps:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Space.</strong> The Director only tracks relative positions like "near" and "inside". A real 3D
      representation of the scene, perhaps through a game engine, would allow much richer spatial reasoning.</li>
    <li><strong>Subtle emotions.</strong> Implicit emotional shifts are still hard to carry across text, pictures and
      sound. Characters with more psychological depth would help.</li>
    <li><strong>Long stories.</strong> Keeping a long story coherent still benefits from a person keeping an eye on
      it. Better memory and planning would reduce that.</li>
    <li><strong>Consistent pictures.</strong> Personalised image generation, such as
      DreamBooth<a href="#ref-dreambooth" class="cite">[5]</a> or textual
      inversion<a href="#ref-ti" class="cite">[6]</a>, could keep characters and places the same across
      scenes.</li>
    <li><strong>Voices.</strong> The system creates soundscapes but no speech. Expressive speech synthesis would let
      the characters talk.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="references">References</h2>
  <ol class="refs">
    <li id="ref-aw">S. Ghorbani.
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.21893">Aether Weaver: Multimodal Affective Narrative Co-Generation with Dynamic Scene Graphs</a>. arXiv:2507.21893, 2025.</li>
    <li id="ref-arif">S. Arif, T. Arif, M. S. Haroon, A. J. Khan, A. A. Raza, A. Athar.
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.11261">The Art of Storytelling: Multi-Agent Generative AI for Dynamic Multimodal Narratives</a>. arXiv:2409.11261, 2024.</li>
    <li id="ref-seed">S. Yang, Y. Ge, Y. Li, Y. Chen, Y. Ge, Y. Shan, Y. Chen.
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.08683">SEED-Story: Multimodal Long Story Generation with Large Language Model</a>. arXiv:2407.08683, 2024.</li>
    <li id="ref-johnson">J. Johnson, A. Gupta, L. Fei-Fei.
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.01622">Image Generation from Scene Graphs</a>. CVPR 2018.</li>
    <li id="ref-dreambooth">N. Ruiz, Y. Li, V. Jampani, Y. Pritch, M. Rubinstein, K. Aberman.
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.12242">DreamBooth: Fine Tuning Text-to-Image Diffusion Models for Subject-Driven Generation</a>. CVPR 2023.</li>
    <li id="ref-ti">R. Gal, Y. Alaluf, Y. Atzmon, O. Patashnik, A. H. Bermano, G. Chechik, D. Cohen-Or.
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.01618">An Image is Worth One Word: Personalizing Text-to-Image Generation using Textual Inversion</a>. arXiv:2208.01618, 2022.</li>
  </ol>

